one of the central themes of  architectural history is the way in  which architecture relates to its own  representation whether this is through  visual material its conventions  orthography perspective or whether it is  through language I want to focus today  on a period in the late 18th century and  on a group of architects called the  visionary architects visionary because  not only did they rethink architectures  relationship to representation to words  and to images but also because they  began to invent utopia we're going to  look at the works of her to do and  especially of itchin we boules and what  eventually was called Oxitec job  outlawed or speaking architecture so I  want to begin here with one of these  visionary architects by the name of  Claude Nicola LeDoux he is someone who  was probably best known today for having  built the first modern Factory and this  Factory was conceived as a place to  manufacture salt in the late 18th  century and I want to start there  because I want to make a point about one  of the more curious aspects of 18th  century architecture today when we think  of the architectural process an  architect has an idea they set it down  on paper they will build a model they  move through a sequence of highly  conventionalized representations the  plan the section the elevation sometimes  they will come up with images that are  more perspectival that evoke somehow a  real through the image and lastly if  they're lucky the building gets built  one of the strange things about the late  18th century is that you'll see  architects reverse the process they will  go through the Commission they will  build a building and from the real they  will imagine a fictional architecture so  for example if we take the Salt Works  which today actually received the status  a world heritage monument through UNESCO  it exists it was built it functioned as  a factory in the late 18th and into the  19th century but what clued Nicola did  is that he took that Factory and he  imagined an ideal city he imagined a  utopia that he called the ideal city of  show so I want to grapple with this idea  in the late enlightenment but I want to  do so by going back in time and looking  at a broad range of examples and in fact  the tradition of the ideal city so let's  begin by looking at another book a book  that Ludo published in 1804 towards the  end of his life as a kind of opus to his  own work he entitled it architecture  considered in relation to the arts to  social mores and to legislation and in  this book you will find a map and a  perspectival view of the ideal city of  show that he extrapolated from his Salt  Works Factory now these are two  different visual strategies a map is  topographic the second is really a  bird's eye perspective as if we were  flying over the town and seeing it  unfold into the countryside but if we  compare them there are other differences  that strike us so when you look at the  map what you see is a highly geometric  configuration it's strongly felt in this  cartographic setting and the theatres of  production of the production of salt is  sitting right in the middle here in a  fully enclosed heavily circumscribed  oval it's as if the town were actually  fortified and in fact it is the ramparts  are indicated in the plan and even in  the legend we see that the salt works  are identified at the heart of it all we  learn from the list on the right the  insert here that a parish church appears  on the right to the left there's a Hall  of Justice  oval shape is so circumscribed that it  actually casts a shadow on the landscape  it's a highly paranoid configuration as  though to get into the city is an act of  enclosure so we have only two roads to  get in and out of town and the sense  that these roads are highly sort of a  diagrammatic we have one road running  north to south from the forest to the  river and then another running east-west  these are the only points of access into  the city of show however if you look at  the perspective of view the view  perspective done as you - oh you're  gonna see an entirely different kind of  experience here the topography unveils  and reveals mountains in the background  you'll see that there's a far more open  sense the ramparts are in fact gone and  and kind of integration of the landscape  with the town so the kind of stifling  geometric character of the map is  replaced by a far more open feel the  semicircle just like the map is sort of  fully elaborated into its elliptical  form but there's a far more seamless  movement between the center the focal  point the Saltworks  and the farms that dot the countryside  now what's clear in the view and less  clear in the map is that Ladue is  actually working out a design for public  space for rest publica and trying to  depict a harmonious living environment  so we see troops gathering onto the  Esplanade we see the factory smoke  rising out of the chimneys to sort of  indicate that production is happening in  a sense activities unfold here and  emphasize that life thrives as a result  of this ideal city and as a result also  of the proper exploitation of nature's  resources so when we look here it's the  buildings that speak not the legend you  have two large public squares  semicircular you see that there's a  church to the west  a market to the east if you look to the  north you will see on the one hand  public baths and a stock exchange and if  you look below the church there's a  structure with a kind of semicircular  auditorium this is actually part of the  university that he is imagining here  it's an auditorium for a medical school  the other thing that's clear as you move  out to the countryside is that he's  mixing old and new structures kind of  neo Palladian villas and old farms old  churches what's happening here is that  the Saltworks the real Salt Works have  generated a vision of an ideal society  it's a utopian gesture representation  comes after the built structure to  generate perfect social world and here  the geometry is what's capturing us all  of its variations the semicircle the  oval the ellipse all of this becomes a  kind of formal metaphor for the harmony  of human existence with nature you can  even evoke the elliptical course of the  planets or think about the economic  metaphor of the circle of production  this is utopia in the deuce terms and so  let's just wrap this up by quoting  LeDoux himself and understanding the  reason for which he looks at the  circular model to generate society this  is Lou do everything is circular in  nature the stone that falls into the  water propagates infinite circles  centripetal force is incessantly  countered by a rotary motion the air the  Seas move in perpetual circles so the  town of show is an immense circle with a  form as pure as that described by the  Sun the line intersecting the diameter  of the Saltworks  crosses the Lu River immense Plains the  town the forest the Geneva Cannell the  Swiss pastures to the left lie the great  rivers the Port of Antwerp and the North  Seas  carry the first and much desired fruits  of our labor Commerce and arts to the  very deserts of Siberia  it's an extraordinary quote and I what I  find most moving in this is the dream of  the global reach of this tiny little  utopia in the countryside which is  generated by a Salt Works Factory and in  this language is this insistence on  geometric forms on nature and on the  rewriting of society so what I'm going  to do now is discuss the repercussions  of all of this but also its sources so I  want to think about the roots of  LeDoux's imagine society by actually  turning back the clock quite a bit going  back to the Renaissance where we see the  visual tradition of utopia emerging in  geometric terms and also in literature  and then I'll sit on the drawings of one  of LeDoux's great contemporaries the  visionary architect  it's yin we brulee who is most often  used to represent architectures  relationship to language in other words  what we call a she dick to appellant or  speaking architecture  you
 

